 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Volume Data Loading and Normalization](2157-volume-data-loading-and-normalization.md)
-Implements a C++ class for loading 3D/4D volumetric data from `.npy` files, normalizing scalar values, and managing frame indices for time-varying volumes. It differs from sibling methods like the Raw Volume Data Reader and DICOM loader by strictly relying on the NumPy binary format and applying a specific min-subtraction normalization strategy rather than standard min-max scaling to $[0, 1]$. It solves the problem of ingesting pre-saved NumPy volume arrays into a rendering pipeline while ensuring consistent data types and value ranges.
+This method implements a C++ class for loading 3D and 4D volumetric data from `.npy` files, normalizing the scalar values, and managing frame indices for time-varying volumes. It strictly relies on the NumPy binary format and applies a min-subtraction normalization strategy ($v' = v - v_{min}$) rather than standard min-max scaling to $[0, 1]$. It ingests pre-saved NumPy volume arrays into a rendering pipeline while ensuring consistent data types and value ranges.
 
 ### [Raw Volume Data Reader](2259-raw-volume-data-reader.md)
-The Raw Volume Data Reader loads raw binary volume data files directly into memory. It parses a fixed header, reads voxel values, applies density clamping and normalization, and maps the data to 3D coordinates. It differs from generic loaders by handling coordinate mapping and density scaling directly during the load phase rather than treating the data as opaque bytes.
+The Raw Volume Data Reader ingests raw binary volume data (typically from CT scanners or specific model formats) into memory, applying domain-specific quantization to reduce precision, and preparing the data for GPU transfer or further processing. It solves the problem of converting high-precision raw sensor data (e.g., 12-bit Hounsfield units) into a format suitable for real-time raycasting or sparse voxel octree construction, often involving thresholding and bit-depth reduction. It differs from generic loaders by embedding specific quantization logic (scaling and thresholding) directly into the loading pipeline rather than treating data as opaque bytes.
 
